# TODO - Host and asset safety

## Objective

Protect the host from malformed or malicious user-supplied data.

## Acceptance

- Test archive traversal, symlinks, oversized resources and bad saves.
- Use synthetic fixtures and reject execution of imported binaries.
- Constrain mods, network inputs, file access and privilege requirements.

## Evidence

Partial evidence, 2026-11-24: fresh validation passed in run
1795575348175824037-2468367 (70 tests).

`games_assets::archive` rejects synthetic traversal and Windows-drive paths,
link/special archive members, executable extensions and oversize entries.
Portable path checks also reject device aliases, control characters,
ambiguous trailing dots/spaces and overlong paths or components.
Directory members must declare zero payload bytes.

The constant-memory archive budget reserves declared uncompressed bytes and
member counts before processing. Synthetic tests verify aggregate exhaustion,
zero-length members, atomic admission failures, exact-length streamed data,
truncation and persistent rejection after an overrun.

SHA-256 save envelopes detect truncated, oversized, tampered or invalid
versions, using strict caller-provided limits. All truncated save prefixes
are also rejected. Network message budgets are enforced separately by
`games::network`.

The Linux native platform now stages decoded archive members in a private
owner-only directory, using Nix descriptor-relative operations, exclusive
file creation and no-follow opens. An owner-controlled, non-group-writable
parent and trusted ancestors are required. The staging registry rejects
duplicate, case-insensitive and file/directory collisions. Portable ASCII
paths and a depth cap of 64 are enforced; implicit directories also consume
the archive entry budget. An error permanently aborts the transaction;
drop attempts cleanup, and no partial result is returned for conversion.

Seven native synthetic tests exercise private permissions, successful
staging and cleanup, malformed metadata and decoded sizes, exhausted
budgets, existing symlinks and hardlinks, collisions and untrusted parents.
Fresh workspace validation passed in run
1795577220441162807-2787138 (93 tests).

Real archive-format decoding, publication, Android device verification and
mod/process sandboxing remain unverified. The trusted parent assumption does
not protect against another process acting as the same user. Actual host
and game adapters must integrate and test those constraints before task
completion.

The canonical manifest now verifies non-executing media replacements against
an explicit trusted title allowlist and the existing base asset descriptors.
Unknown or unapproved keys, game-data replacements, incompatible dimensions,
PCM layouts or lengths, and mismatched payload hashes are rejected. No files
are opened or installed by this policy; it is not a process sandbox or a
replacement for title-specific data validation. Synthetic image and audio
fixtures passed in run 1795578114804270643-2943036 (121 tests).

The native bundle reader now admits one explicitly selected passive-media
overlay over two pinned canonical manifests. Every replacement descriptor
must pass the existing title allowlist and layout policy before any
overlay is returned. The base and replacement must name the same title.
Each read verifies the selected bundle's regular-file, size and digest
constraints, and a failed replacement cannot silently fall back to the
base. Synthetic native tests preserve non-replaced data, reject mixed
approved/unapproved records, incompatible formats and cross-title
manifests, and detect tampering and symlinks. Fresh validation passed in
run 1795584472390696736-3793042. No executable or gameplay-data mods,
automatic mod discovery, revision-compatibility claim or process sandbox
are supplied by this adapter.

Native save slots now reuse the portable SHA-256 envelope and bounded
descriptor-relative file admission used by asset storage. Numeric slot IDs
are mapped to fixed filenames inside a pinned, owner-controlled directory.
Writes use an exclusive owner-only temporary file, sync its contents, then
atomically replace the slot. Directory sync is reported separately because
its failure can occur after the new save becomes visible. Save encoding
also handles reservation failures and exposes the encoded byte limit.

Synthetic tests cover portable decoding, empty and independent slots,
preserving a previous save on rejection, wrong checksums, oversized files,
symlinks, hardlinks, FIFOs, untrusted permissions, unpublished-file cleanup
and a renamed directory handle. Fresh validation passed in run
1795587630637948131-8605. Actual power-loss recovery and Android storage
remain untested; trusted ancestors and same-user processes are assumed.

A synchronized two-publisher save test verifies that both writers can
replace the same slot, that the final bytes decode as exactly one of
the complete payloads, and that no staging files remain. Both directory
durability receipts succeeded on the Linux test filesystem. This is
last-publication-wins evidence, not a physical power-loss experiment.

Manifest JSON now admits records individually within the caller's count
limit, using a borrowed raw array and the existing Serde JSON parser.
The encoded byte limit is enforced before output growth, with fallible
capacity reservation shared between the record and byte vectors. Tests
cover rejection before decoding an excess malformed record, exact
count and byte limits, and unchanged version-one serialized bytes.
The input remains bounded by the original manifest byte limit; this
does not establish a sandbox or independently authenticate metadata.

The canonical bundle and save readers now share the metadata-only inode
pin used for source snapshots. A file must pass admission before it is
opened for content through trusted kernel procfs; the reopened descriptor
must still have the same identity and length. This avoids a second
lookup of the untrusted filename. A previously pinned file may have
zero links after atomic replacement; additional hardlinks remain
inadmissible. Tests pin a prior file across a rename and interleave
128 save loads with 32 atomic replacements, requiring complete valid
payloads and no staging residue. Native reads now require trusted
procfs; Android and power-loss behavior are not established.

The display preference writer also verifies any existing slot as valid
portable display settings before replacing it. Direct use of the writer
can no longer silently overwrite an unrelated game-progress payload or a
corrupted save envelope. Existing valid preferences retain atomic
replacement and first-use behavior. The dedicated-directory contract and
trusted same-user writer assumption remain; this is not compare-and-swap.

The shared native descriptor reader also rechecks modification time, length,
identity and admission policy after the bounded read. A deterministic fixture
rewrites a three-byte file in place and changes its modification timestamp;
the reader rejects the stale observation. Atomic replacement still permits
reading the unchanged, pinned previous inode. This detects observable changes,
not all races or deliberate timestamp restoration by a same-user writer.

A dedicated credential snapshot reuses this reader while requiring effective
user ownership, no group or other permission bits and no additional hard
links. Synthetic checks reject public modes, symlinks, hard links and oversize
credentials while accepting an owner-only file. Trusted ancestor and procfs
assumptions remain; the caller owns parsing and credential memory lifetime.

Flat ZIP imports now admit all member names, metadata and declared sizes
against the aggregate budget before decoding the first payload. This uses
read-only central metadata from the existing ZIP parser, retaining its full
local-header consistency checks. Synthetic fixtures demonstrate that a late
executable name, case collision or aggregate overflow prevents any callback;
a deliberately corrupted first payload does not mask later unsafe metadata.
The source byte limit still bounds the central-directory parser's input;
its metadata allocation is not independently limited by the member count.
